{"step_index":10050,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T14:06:04Z","thinking":"Extraction from the Excel file succeeded with a high item count, indicating the base extraction process functions correctly. The application must now process several PDF files. These PDFs represent warehouse data which requires parsing.\n\n\nThe error log clearly indicates a concurrency limit within the processing pipeline; multiple threads concurrently hit the same web gateway. This concurrent access causes the server to return \"Server busy\" errors due to the single browser tab limitation in the current architecture. One thread succeeded while the other five failed due to this concurrency limit.\n\n\nThe UI issue centers on the loading pop-up's unexpected behavior across different application sections. The pop-up, defined in the HTML structure, is likely styled with a full-screen overlay positioning, causing it to persist across pages. Examining the CSS should reveal whether this modal uses `position: fixed` or `position: absolute` for overlay functionality.\n\nRegarding the first issue, a potential concurrency conflict arises from the concurrent access initiated by parallel workers; thus, it is a single-tab limitation. The code within the file handler must be assessed to understand if the calls can execute concurrently within a single tab.\n\n\nThe current parallel processing approach is incompatible with the single-browser-tab limitation for vision extraction. Sequential processing of PDF files, either via a loop or a locking mechanism (mutex/semaphore), is the correct solution. The existing retry logic with a fixed 2-second sleep is insufficient; a backoff strategy is needed to handle 429 errors from the vision gateway, especially when multiple files are processed.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"d:\\AI_Engineer\\Pharmacy-agy-vision\\desktop_app\\templates\\index.html","EndLine":1955,"StartLine":1925,"t